        [Turn 5 REASONING] (1596 chars)
        For the three dice let the first roll be \((a,b,c)\) with each value in \(\{1,\dots ,6\}\).

Kevin must keep exactly one die and re‑roll the other two.  
If he keeps a die showing \(k\), the two new dice must sum to \(7-k\).

The number of ordered pairs \((x,y)\) with \(x+y=s\) ( \(x,y\in\{1,\dots ,6\}\) ) is  

\[
\#(s)=\begin{cases}
s-1,&2\le s\le 7,\\[2pt]
13-s,&7\le s\le 12,\\[2pt]
0,&\text{otherwise}.
\end{cases}
\]

Thus for a kept value \(k\),

\[
\#(7-k)=\begin{cases}
6-k,&1\le k\le 6,\\
0,&k=6\text{ gives }7-k=1.
\end{cases}
\]

Hence the probability of obtaining a total of \(7\) after re‑rolling the two dice is  

\[
\frac{\#(7-k)}{36}= \frac{6-k}{36}.
\]

To maximise this probability Kevin will keep the die with the **smallest** face value,
because \(6-k\) is decreasing in \(k\).  
Let \(m=\min\{a,b,c\}\). Then the maximal number of favourable ordered pairs is  

\[
\max\# = 6-m .
\]

---

### Summing over all first rolls

The required overall probability is

\[
p=\frac1{216}\sum_{a,b,c}\frac{6-\min\{a,b,c\}}{36}
   =\frac{1}{216\cdot 36}\sum_{a,b,c}\bigl(6-\min\{a,b,c\}\bigr).
\]

Count triples whose minimum equals \(m\) (\(1\le m\le6\)):

\[
\#\{\,\min=m\,\}= (7-m)^3-(6-m)^3\qquad (m<6),\qquad
\#\{\,\min=6\,\}=1 .
\]

Therefore

\[
\begin{aligned}
\sum_{a,b,c}\bigl(6-\min\{a,b,c\}\bigr)
&=\sum_{m=1}^{6}(6-m)\bigl[(7-m)^3-(6-m)^3\bigr] \\
&=5\cdot91+4\cdot61+3\cdot37+2\cdot19+1\cdot7+0\cdot1\\
&=855 .
\end{aligned}
\]

Consequently  

\[
p=\frac{855}{216\cdot36}= \frac{95}{864},
\qquad
216p=\frac{855}{36}= \frac{95}{4}=23.75 .
\]

\[
\boxed{\dfrac{95}{4}}
\]

