        For each initial result \((a,b,c)\) Kevin may keep one die and re‑roll the other two.  
If he keeps the die showing \(k\), the sum after the re‑roll will be  

\[
k+X+Y,
\]

where \(X,Y\) are independent uniform numbers from \(1\) to \(6\).
Hence the probability of ending with total \(7\) is  

\[
\Pr (X+Y = 7-k)=\frac{N(7-k)}{36},
\]

where \(N(s)\) is the number of ordered pairs \((x,y)\) with \(x+y=s\).

\[
N(s)=\begin{cases}
s-1 & (2\le s\le 7),\\[2pt]
13-s & (7\le s\le 12),\\[2pt]
0 & \text{otherwise}.
\end{cases}
\]

For a given triple \((a,b,c)\) Kevin chooses the die that maximises \(N(7-k)\).
Thus the optimal conditional probability for that triple is  

\[
\frac{\max\{N(7-a),\,N(7-b),\,N(7-c)\}}{36}.
\]

Summing this over all \(6^{3}=216\) ordered triples gives

\[
\sum_{a,b,c} \max\{N(7-a),N(7-b),N(7-c)\}=855 .
\]

Hence the overall probability when exactly two dice are re‑thrown is  

\[
p=\frac{1}{216}\cdot\frac{855}{36}= \frac{95}{864}.
\]

Finally  

\[
216\,p = 216\cdot\frac{95}{864}= \frac{95}{4}=23.75 .
\]

