        The optimal strategy when Kevin must re‑roll exactly two dice is to keep the die showing the smallest value, because the probability of completing the sum 7 after re‑rolling the other two dice is  

\[
f(k)=\frac{\#\{(x,y):x+y=7-k\}}{36}
      =\frac{6-k}{36}\qquad(k=1,\dots ,5),\qquad f(6)=0 .
\]

Hence for an initial triple \((a,b,c)\) the success probability is \(f(\min\{a,b,c\})\).

Let \(M=\min\{a,b,c\}\).  
The distribution of \(M\) for three fair dice is  

\[
\begin{aligned}
P(M=1)&=\frac{91}{216},\\
P(M=2)&=\frac{61}{216},\\
P(M=3)&=\frac{37}{216},\\
P(M=4)&=\frac{19}{216},\\
P(M=5)&=\frac{7}{216},\\
P(M=6)&=\frac{1}{216}.
\end{aligned}
\]

Therefore  

\[
\begin{aligned}
p &=\sum_{m=1}^{5} f(m)\,P(M=m)\\
  &=\frac{5}{36}\frac{91}{216}
    +\frac{4}{36}\frac{61}{216}
    +\frac{3}{36}\frac{37}{216}
    +\frac{2}{36}\frac{19}{216}
    +\frac{1}{36}\frac{7}{216}\\[2mm]
  &=\frac{95}{864}.
\end{aligned}
\]

Finally  

\[
216p = 216\cdot\frac{95}{864}= \frac{95}{4}=23.75 .
\]

\[
\boxed{\dfrac{95}{4}}
\]

