        [Turn 6 REASONING] (1055 chars)
        The optimal strategy when exactly two dice may be re‑thrown is to keep the smallest die, because the probability that the two new dice sum to the needed value \(7-k\) is  

\[
P_{k}= \frac{\#\{(x,y):x+y=7-k\}}{36}= \frac{6-k}{36},
\qquad k=1,\dots ,6 .
\]

Hence, for a given initial triple \((a,b,c)\) the success probability is
\[
\max_{k\in\{a,b,c\}} P_{k}=P_{\min(a,b,c)}.
\]

Let \(M=\min(a,b,c)\).  
The distribution of \(M\) for three independent fair dice is  

\[
\begin{aligned}
P(M=1)&=\frac{91}{216},\\
P(M=2)&=\frac{61}{216},\\
P(M=3)&=\frac{37}{216},\\
P(M=4)&=\frac{19}{216},\\
P(M=5)&=\frac{7}{216},\\
P(M=6)&=\frac{1}{216}.
\end{aligned}
\]

Therefore the overall probability of ending with a total of \(7\) is  

\[
\begin{aligned}
p&=\sum_{m=1}^{6} P(M=m)\,P_{m}
   =\sum_{m=1}^{6} \frac{6-m}{36}\,P(M=m)\\[2mm]
  &=\frac{91\cdot5+61\cdot4+37\cdot3+19\cdot2+7\cdot1}{36\cdot216}
   =\frac{855}{7776}
   =\frac{95}{864}.
\end{aligned}
\]

Finally  

\[
216\,p = 216\cdot\frac{95}{864}= \frac{95}{4}=23.75.
\]

\[
\boxed{\dfrac{95}{4}}
\]

