        [Turn 8 REASONING] (989 chars)
        The optimal strategy when Kevin must re‑roll exactly two dice is to keep the die showing the smallest value.  
For a kept value \(v\) the probability that the two new dice sum to \(7-v\) is  

\[
\frac{N(7-v)}{36},
\qquad 
N(s)=\begin{cases}
s-1 & 2\le s\le 7,\\[2pt]
13-s & 7\le s\le 12,\\[2pt]
0 & \text{otherwise}.
\end{cases}
\]

Thus the probabilities for \(v=1,2,3,4,5,6\) are respectively
\(\frac{5}{36},\frac{4}{36},\frac{3}{36},\frac{2}{36},\frac{1}{36},0\).

Let \(C(m)\) be the number of ordered triples of dice whose minimum equals \(m\):
\[
C(m)=(7-m)^3-(6-m)^3\quad (m=1,\dots ,5),\qquad C(6)=1.
\]
Hence  

\[
\begin{aligned}
\sum_{\text{all triples}} N(7-\min)&=
91\cdot5+61\cdot4+37\cdot3+19\cdot2+7\cdot1+1\cdot0\\
&=855 .
\end{aligned}
\]

There are \(6^3\cdot6^2=7776\) equally likely outcomes (initial roll and the two re‑rolled dice).  
Therefore  

\[
p=\frac{855}{7776}=\frac{95}{864},
\qquad
216p=\frac{216\cdot95}{864}=\frac{95}{4}=23.75 .
\]

\[
\boxed{23.75}
\]

