        [Turn 12 REASONING] (2658 chars)
        The domain  

\[
\min\{|x-2|,|x+2|\}+|y|\le 1
\]

splits into two convex “diamonds’’  

\[
\begin{aligned}
D_{L}&=\{(x,y):|x+2|+|y|\le 1,\;x\le 0\},\\
D_{R}&=\{(x,y):|x-2|+|y|\le 1,\;x\ge 0\}.
\end{aligned}
\]

Hence  

\[
S=\Bigl\{\frac{2y-3}{2x-1}\;:\;(x,y)\in D_{L}\cup D_{R}\Bigr\}.
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 1.  Image of the right diamond \(D_{R}\)

The four edges of \(D_{R}\) are  

\[
\begin{array}{ll}
E_{1}: y=x-1 , & 1\le x\le 2,\\[2pt]
E_{2}: y=-x+3 ,& 2\le x\le 3,\\[2pt]
E_{3}: y=x-3 , & 2\le x\le 3,\\[2pt]
E_{4}: y=-x+1 , & 1\le x\le 2 .
\end{array}
\]

On each edge we substitute \(y\) into  

\[
f(x,y)=\frac{2y-3}{2x-1}
\]

and obtain a one‑variable rational function:

\[
\begin{aligned}
f_{E_1}(x)&=\frac{2x-5}{2x-1},\\
f_{E_2}(x)&=\frac{-2x+3}{2x-1},\\
f_{E_3}(x)&=\frac{2x-9}{2x-1},\\
f_{E_4}(x)&=\frac{1-2x}{2x-1}\equiv-1 .
\end{aligned}
\]

Each derivative is non‑zero on the corresponding interval, so the
extrema occur at the endpoints (the vertices).  
Evaluating at the four vertices gives  

\[
f(1,0)=-3,\qquad f(2,1)=-\frac13,\qquad
f(3,0)=-\frac35,\qquad f(2,-1)=-\frac53 .
\]

Hence  

\[
f(D_{R})=[-3,\,-\tfrac13].
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 2.  Image of the left diamond \(D_{L}\)

The edges of \(D_{L}\) are  

\[
\begin{array}{ll}
L_{1}: y=x+3 , & -3\le x\le -2,\\[2pt]
L_{2}: y=-x-1 ,& -2\le x\le -1,\\[2pt]
L_{3}: y=x+1 , & -2\le x\le -1,\\[2pt]
L_{4}: y=-x-3 , & -3\le x\le -2 .
\end{array}
\]

Substituting gives

\[
\begin{aligned}
f_{L_1}(x)&=\frac{2x+3}{2x-1},\\
f_{L_2}(x)&=\frac{-2x-5}{2x-1},\\
f_{L_3}(x)&\equiv 1,\\
f_{L_4}(x)&=\frac{-2x-9}{2x-1}.
\end{aligned}
\]

Again the derivatives never vanish inside the intervals, so the
extrema lie at the vertices.  The four vertex values are  

\[
f(-3,0)=\frac37,\qquad f(-2,1)=\frac15,\qquad
f(-1,0)=1,\qquad f(-2,-1)=1 .
\]

Consequently  

\[
f(D_{L})=\Bigl[\frac15,\,1\Bigr].
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 3.  The whole set \(S\)

\[
S = f(D_{L})\cup f(D_{R})
    =\Bigl[-3,\,-\frac13\Bigr]\;\cup\;
      \Bigl[\frac15,\,1\Bigr].
\]

The two intervals are disjoint; the “length’’ of a set is the sum of the
lengths of its connected components:

\[
\begin{aligned}
\text{length}(S) &=
\Bigl(-\frac13-(-3)\Bigr)+\Bigl(1-\frac15\Bigr)\\[2pt]
&= \frac{8}{3}+\frac{4}{5}
   =\frac{40}{15}+\frac{12}{15}
   =\frac{52}{15}.
\end{aligned}
\]

Thus the length of \(S\) is \(\displaystyle\frac{52}{15}\).
Writing \(\frac{m}{p}\) in lowest terms gives \(m=52,\;p=15\) (they are
coprime).  

\[
\boxed{m+p = 52+15 = 67}.
\]

