\end{pmatrix}
\]

has size \((n-1)\times (n-1)\).  
For \(i=1,\dots ,n-1\) the diagonal entry equals  

\[
(i+1)^{3}-(i+1)=i(i+1)(i+2).
\]

Hence  

\[
M_n(\lambda)=\operatorname{diag}\bigl(a_1,\dots ,a_{n-1}\bigr)-\lambda J,
\qquad 
a_i=i(i+1)(i+2),
\]

where \(J\) is the all‑ones matrix (rank 1).

---

### Determinant

Using the matrix determinant lemma  

\[
\det(D-\lambda J)=\det(D)\bigl(1-\lambda\mathbf 1^{\!T}D^{-1}\mathbf 1\bigr),
\]

with \(D=\operatorname{diag}(a_i)\) and \(\mathbf 1\) the vector of ones, we obtain  

\[
\det M_n(\lambda)=\Bigl(\prod_{i=1}^{n-1}a_i\Bigr)
\Bigl(1-\lambda\sum_{i=1}^{n-1}\frac{1}{a_i}\Bigr).
\]

Since the product is never zero, \(\det M_n(\lambda)=0\) iff  

\[
1-\lambda\sum_{i=1}^{n-1}\frac{1}{i(i+1)(i+2)}=0,
\qquad\Longrightarrow\qquad
\lambda_n=\frac{1}{\displaystyle\sum_{i=1}^{n-1}\frac{1}{i(i+1)(i+2)}} .
\]

---

### Evaluating the sum  

\[
\frac{1}{i(i+1)(i+2)}=
\frac12\Bigl(\frac{1}{i(i+1)}-\frac{1}{(i+1)(i+2)}\Bigr),
\]

so the series telescopes:

\[
\begin{aligned}
S_n&=\sum_{i=1}^{n-1}\frac{1}{i(i+1)(i+2)}\\
   &=\frac12\Bigl(\frac{1}{1\cdot2}-\frac{1}{n(n+1)}\Bigr)
   =\frac14-\frac{1}{2n(n+1)} .
\end{aligned}
\]

Therefore  

\[
\lambda_n=\frac{1}{S_n}
        =\frac{1}{\displaystyle\frac14-\frac{1}{2n(n+1)}}
        =\frac{4n(n+1)}{n(n+1)-2}.
\]

---

### Limit superior  

\[
\lambda_n=\frac{4n(n+1)}{n(n+1)-2}
        =4\;\frac{1}{1-\dfrac{2}{n(n+1)}}\xrightarrow[n\to\infty]{}4 .
\]

The sequence \(\{\lambda_n\}\) is decreasing and converges to \(4\); hence  

\[
\boxed{I=\limsup_{n\to\infty}\lambda_n=4}.
\]

