        For  

\[
M_n(\lambda)=\begin{pmatrix}
a_1-\lambda &-\lambda &\dots &-\lambda\\
-\lambda &a_2-\lambda &\dots &-\lambda\\
\vdots &\vdots &\ddots &\vdots\\
-\lambda &-\lambda &\dots &a_{n-1}-\lambda
\end{pmatrix},
\qquad 
a_k=k(k+1)(k+2)\;(k=1,\dots ,n-1)
\]

the off–diagonal entries are all \(-\lambda\).  
Let  

\[
A_n=\operatorname{diag}(a_1,\dots ,a_{n-1}),\qquad 
J_{n-1}= \mathbf 1\mathbf 1^{\!T}
\]

(\(\mathbf 1\) is the column vector of ones).  
Then  

\[
M_n(\lambda)=A_n-\lambda J_{n-1}=A_n+(-\lambda)\,\mathbf 1\mathbf 1^{\!T}.
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### Determinant

Since \(A_n\) is diagonal and invertible, we can use the matrix‑determinant lemma  

\[
\det(A+u v^{\!T})=\det(A)\bigl(1+v^{\!T}A^{-1}u\bigr).
\]

With \(u=v=\mathbf 1\) and the scalar \(-\lambda\),

\[
\det\bigl(A_n-\lambda J_{n-1}\bigr)=\det(A_n)\Bigl(1-\lambda\,
\mathbf 1^{\!T}A_n^{-1}\mathbf 1\Bigr).
\]

Because \(A_n^{-1}=\operatorname{diag}\!\bigl(\frac1{a_1},\dots ,\frac1{a_{n-1}}\bigr)\),

\[
\mathbf 1^{\!T}A_n^{-1}\mathbf 1=\sum_{k=1}^{n-1}\frac1{a_k}
      =\sum_{k=1}^{n-1}\frac1{k(k+1)(k+2)} .
\]

Hence the determinant vanishes exactly when  

\[
1-\lambda\sum_{k=1}^{n-1}\frac1{k(k+1)(k+2)}=0,
\qquad\Longrightarrow\qquad
\boxed{\;\lambda_n=
\frac{1}{\displaystyle\sum_{k=1}^{n-1}\frac1{k(k+1)(k+2)}}\; } .
\]

Since the sum in the denominator is increasing with \(n\), \(\lambda_n\) is a decreasing positive sequence; the maximal \(\lambda\) that makes the determinant zero is this unique root.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### Limit of \(\lambda_n\)

The series can be telescoped:

\[
\frac{1}{k(k+1)(k+2)}
      =\frac12\Bigl(\frac1{k(k+1)}-\frac1{(k+1)(k+2)}\Bigr).
\]

Thus for \(N\ge 1\)

\[
\sum_{k=1}^{N}\frac1{k(k+1)(k+2)}
   =\frac12\Bigl(\frac1{1\cdot2}-\frac1{(N+1)(N+2)}\Bigr)
   =\frac14-\frac{1}{2(N+1)(N+2)}.
\]

Letting \(N=n-1\to\infty\),

\[
\sum_{k=1}^{\infty}\frac1{k(k+1)(k+2)}=\frac14 .
\]

Consequently  

\[
\lim_{n\to\infty}\lambda_n
   =\frac{1}{\displaystyle\sum_{k=1}^{\infty}\frac1{k(k+1)(k+2)}}
   =\frac{1}{\frac14}=4 .
\]

Because \(\lambda_n\) is decreasing, \(\displaystyle\limsup_{n\to\infty}\lambda_n\) equals this limit.

\[
\boxed{I=4}
\]

