        [Turn 3 REASONING] (1723 chars)
        \[
M_n(\lambda)=\operatorname{diag}\bigl(2^{3}-2,\;3^{3}-3,\dots ,n^{3}-n\bigr)-\lambda J_{\,n-1},
\qquad J_{\,n-1}= \mathbf 1\mathbf 1^{\!T}
\]

where \(\mathbf 1\) is the \((n-1)\)-vector of ones.  
Since \(J_{\,n-1}\) has rank 1 we can use the matrix‑determinant lemma.

---

### 1.  Determinant

Let \(D_n=\operatorname{diag}(k^{3}-k)_{k=2}^{n}\).  
Then  

\[
\det\bigl(D_n-\lambda J_{\,n-1}\bigr)
   =\det(D_n)\,\det\!\bigl(I-\lambda D_n^{-1}J_{\,n-1}\bigr)
   =\det(D_n)\bigl(1-\lambda\,\mathbf 1^{\!T}D_n^{-1}\mathbf 1\bigr).
\]

Because \(D_n\) is invertible (\(k^{3}-k\neq0\) for \(k\ge2\)), the determinant vanishes iff  

\[
1-\lambda\sum_{k=2}^{n}\frac{1}{k^{3}-k}=0 .
\]

Hence the unique root is  

\[
\boxed{\displaystyle 
\lambda_n=\frac{1}{\displaystyle\sum_{k=2}^{n}\frac{1}{k^{3}-k}} } .
\]

---

### 2.  Evaluating the series

\[
\frac{1}{k^{3}-k}= \frac{1}{k(k-1)(k+1)}
               =\frac{1}{2(k+1)}+\frac{1}{2(k-1)}-\frac{1}{k}.
\]

Summing from \(k=2\) to \(n\) gives a telescoping series:

\[
\begin{aligned}
S_n&=\sum_{k=2}^{n}\frac{1}{k^{3}-k}\\
   &=\frac12\!\sum_{k=2}^{n}\!\frac{1}{k+1}
     +\frac12\!\sum_{k=2}^{n}\!\frac{1}{k-1}
     -\sum_{k=2}^{n}\frac{1}{k}\\[2mm]
   &=\frac14-\frac{1}{2n(n+1)} .
\end{aligned}
\]

Therefore  

\[
\lambda_n=\frac{1}{S_n}
        =\frac{1}{\displaystyle\frac14-\frac{1}{2n(n+1)}}
        =\frac{4n(n+1)}{n(n+1)-2}.
\]

---

### 3.  Limit superior

\[
\lambda_n = \frac{4n(n+1)}{n(n+1)-2}
          = 4\;\frac{1}{1-\dfrac{2}{n(n+1)}}\xrightarrow[n\to\infty]{}4 .
\]

The sequence \(\lambda_n\) is decreasing (because \(S_n\) increases), so the
\(\limsup\) equals the ordinary limit.

\[
\boxed{\,I=\displaystyle\limsup_{n\to\infty}\lambda_n = 4\,}.
\]

