        [Turn 18 REASONING] (2161 chars)
        For  

\[
M_n(\lambda)=\bigl(m_{ij}\bigr)_{1\le i,j\le n-1},\qquad 
m_{ii}=k^{3}-k-\lambda ,\; m_{ij}=-\lambda\;(i\neq j),
\]

where \(k=i+1\) (so the diagonal entries are \(2^{3}-2,3^{3}-3,\dots ,n^{3}-n\)).
Write  

\[
D_n=\operatorname{diag}\bigl(2^{3}-2,\;3^{3}-3,\dots ,n^{3}-n\bigr),\qquad 
J_{n-1}= \mathbf 1\mathbf 1^{\!T}
\]

(\(\mathbf 1\) is the column vector of all ones).  
Then  

\[
M_n(\lambda)=D_n-\lambda J_{n-1}.
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 1.  Determinant

\(D_n\) is invertible because each diagonal entry \(k^{3}-k=k(k-1)(k+1)>0\) for
\(k\ge 2\).  
The matrix‑determinant lemma gives  

\[
\det\bigl(D_n-\lambda J_{n-1}\bigr)=\det(D_n)\,
\Bigl(1-\lambda\,\mathbf 1^{\!T}D_n^{-1}\mathbf 1\Bigr).
\]

Hence \(\det M_n(\lambda)=0\) iff  

\[
1-\lambda\sum_{k=2}^{n}\frac{1}{k^{3}-k}=0 .
\]

Because the factor \(\det(D_n)\neq0\), this is the **only** root of the
determinant.  
Consequently  

\[
\boxed{\displaystyle 
\lambda_n=\frac{1}{\displaystyle\sum_{k=2}^{n}\frac{1}{k^{3}-k}} } .
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 2.  Evaluating the sum

\[
\frac{1}{k^{3}-k}= \frac{1}{k(k-1)(k+1)}
               =\frac{1}{2(k-1)}-\frac{1}{k}+\frac{1}{2(k+1)} .
\]

Summing from \(k=2\) to \(n\) telescopes:

\[
\begin{aligned}
\sum_{k=2}^{n}\frac{1}{k^{3}-k}
&=\frac12\sum_{k=2}^{n}\frac{1}{k-1}
   -\sum_{k=2}^{n}\frac{1}{k}
   +\frac12\sum_{k=2}^{n}\frac{1}{k+1}\\[2mm]
&=\frac12 H_{n-1}-\bigl(H_n-1\bigr)+\frac12\bigl(H_{n+1}-\tfrac32\bigr)\\
&=\frac14-\frac{1}{2n(n+1)},
\end{aligned}
\]

where \(H_m\) denotes the \(m\)-th harmonic number.  
Therefore  

\[
\lambda_n=\frac{1}{\displaystyle\frac14-\frac{1}{2n(n+1)}}
        =\frac{4n(n+1)}{n(n+1)-2}.
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 3.  Limit superior

\[
\lambda_n = \frac{4n(n+1)}{n(n+1)-2}
          = 4\;\frac{1}{1-\dfrac{2}{n(n+1)}}\xrightarrow[n\to\infty]{}4 .
\]

The sequence \(\{\lambda_n\}\) is decreasing for \(n\ge 2\) and converges
to \(4\); hence its lim‑sup equals its limit.

\[
\boxed{I=\displaystyle\limsup_{n\to\infty}\lambda_n = 4 }.
\]

