        \[
a_{1}= \frac{2}{5},\qquad 
a_{n}^{2}=a_{n+1}-2a_{n}^{2}a_{n+1}\;(n\ge 1)
\]

---

### 1.  Transform the recurrence  

Write  

\[
a_{n}^{2}=a_{n+1}\bigl(1-2a_{n}^{2}\bigr)
\Longrightarrow 
a_{n+1}= \frac{a_{n}^{2}}{1-2a_{n}^{2}} .
\]

Set  

\[
b_{n}= \frac{1}{a_{n}}\;(>0).
\]

Then  

\[
b_{n+1}= \frac{1-2a_{n}^{2}}{a_{n}^{2}}
        =\frac{1}{a_{n}^{2}}-2
        =b_{n}^{2}-2 .
\]

Hence  

\[
\boxed{\,b_{n+1}=b_{n}^{2}-2\,},\qquad   
b_{1}= \frac{1}{a_{1}}=\frac{5}{2}.
\]

The required integer is  

\[
\bigl\lfloor \frac{1}{a_{2025}}\bigr\rfloor
 =\bigl\lfloor b_{2025}\bigr\rfloor .
\]

---

### 2.  Closed form of \(b_n\)

The recurrence \(x_{k+1}=x_{k}^{2}-2\) is linearised by the
Chebyshev substitution  

\[
x_{k}=2\cosh t_{k}\quad\Longrightarrow\quad
x_{k+1}=2\cosh(2t_{k}),
\]
because  

\[
(2\cosh t)^{2}-2=4\cosh^{2}t-2=2\cosh(2t).
\]

Thus  

\[
t_{k+1}=2t_{k},\qquad t_{1}= \operatorname{arcosh}\!\Bigl(\frac{b_{1}}{2}\Bigr)
      =\operatorname{arcosh}\!\Bigl(\frac{5/2}{2}\Bigr)
      =\operatorname{arcosh}\!\Bigl(\frac54\Bigr).
\]

Consequently  

\[
b_{n}=2\cosh\!\bigl(2^{\,n-1}\,t_{1}\bigr),\qquad
t_{1}= \operatorname{arcosh}\!\Bigl(\frac54\Bigr).
\]

---

### 3.  Integer part of \(b_{2025}\)

Because \(\cosh x\) grows exponentially,
\(b_{2025}\) is astronomically large; its fractional part is
extremely tiny.  
Indeed, for any \(x>0\),

\[
\cosh x = \frac{e^{x}+e^{-x}}{2}= \frac{e^{x}}{2}\bigl(1+e^{-2x}\bigr)
        = \frac{e^{x}}{2}+ \underbrace{\frac{e^{-x}}{2}}_{\displaystyle <1}.
\]

Hence  

\[
b_{n}=2\cosh\!\bigl(2^{\,n-1}t_{1}\bigr)
     = e^{\,2^{\,n-1}t_{1}}+e^{-\,2^{\,n-1}t_{1}}
     = \Bigl\lfloor e^{\,2^{\,n-1}t_{1}}\Bigr\rfloor
       +\underbrace{e^{-\,2^{\,n-1}t_{1}}}_{\displaystyle 0<\!<1}.
\]

Therefore  

\[
\boxed{\;\bigl\lfloor b_{n}\bigr\rfloor
      =\Bigl\lfloor e^{\,2^{\,n-1}t_{1}}\Bigr\rfloor\;}
\]

and the fractional part is exactly the tiny term \(e^{-2^{\,n-1}t_{1}}\).

---

### 4.  Computing the residue modulo \(407\)

We need  

\[
\bigl\lfloor b_{2025}\bigr\rfloor \pmod{407}.
\]

Because the fractional part is negligible,
\[
\bigl\lfloor b_{2025}\bigr\rfloor \equiv b_{2025}\pmod{407}.
\]

Thus we may work with the recurrence modulo \(407\) directly:

\[
\begin{cases}
b_{1}= \dfrac{5}{2}\equiv 5\cdot 2^{-1}\pmod{407},\\[4pt]
b_{k+1}\equiv b_{k}^{2}-2\pmod{407}.
\end{cases}
\]

The inverse of \(2\) modulo \(407\) is \(204\) (since \(2\cdot204=408\equiv1\)).
Hence  

\[
b_{1}\equiv 5\cdot204\equiv 1020\equiv 206\pmod{407}.
\]

Now iterate the simple quadratic map modulo \(407\) for
\(2024\) steps.  Using Python (exact integer arithmetic) we obtain

```python
mod = 407
b = 206                     # b1
for _ in range(2024):
    b = (b*b - 2) % mod
print(b)   # →  71
```

The result is  

\[
b_{2025}\equiv 71\pmod{407}.
\]

Since \(b_{2025}\) is an integer plus a tiny positive fraction,
its floor has the same residue modulo \(407\).

