        [Turn 37 REASONING] (56892 chars)
        For  

\[
f(x)=\sum_{n\in S_x}\frac1{2^{n}},\qquad 
S_x=\{n\in \mathbb N\;|\;\lfloor nx\rfloor \text{ is even}\},
\qquad 0\le x<1,
\]

let  

\[
a_n(x)=\begin{cases}
1,&\lfloor nx\rfloor\text{ even},\\[2mm]
0,&\lfloor nx\rfloor\text{ odd}.
\end{cases}
\]

Then  

\[
f(x)=\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}\frac{a_n(x)}{2^{n}},\qquad 
a_1(x)=1\;( \text{because }\lfloor x\rfloor =0).
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 1.  A lower bound for \(f(x)\)

For any integer \(k\ge 0\) consider the block  

\[
B_k=\{\,n\mid 1\le n\le 3\cdot 2^{k}\,\}.
\]

Because \(\lfloor (n+2^{k})x\)-\(\lfloor nx\rfloor\le 2^{k}\), the parity of
\(\lfloor nx\rfloor\) can change at most once inside each interval of length
\(2^{k}\).  Consequently, inside the block \(B_k\) at most one third of the
indices can have \(a_n(x)=0\); the other two thirds must have \(a_n(x)=1\).
Hence

\[
\sum_{n=1}^{3\cdot 2^{k}}\frac{a_n(x)}{2^{n}}
\ge
\sum_{j=0}^{2^{k}-1}\frac{1}{2^{3j+1}}
=
\frac{1}{2}\sum_{j=0}^{2^{k}-1}\Bigl(\frac1{8}\Bigr)^{j}
=
\frac{1}{2}\,\frac{1-(1/8)^{\,2^{k}}}{1-1/8}.
\]

Letting \(k\to\infty\) gives  

\[
f(x)\ge \frac{1}{2}\cdot\frac{1}{1-1/8}= \frac{4}{7}\qquad\text{for every }x\in[0,1).
\tag{1}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 2.  A sequence that approaches the bound

Take the dyadic rationals  

\[
x_k=\frac{\bigl\lfloor 2^{\,k+1}/3\bigl\rfloor}{2^{\,k}}\qquad(k=1,2,\dots).
\]

For each \(k\) we have \(0\le x_k<1\) and, because the denominator is a
power of two,  

\[
