        [Turn 25 REASONING] (4627 chars)
        \[
\text{Find the smallest }n\ge 866\text{ for which a bijection }f:\{1,\dots ,n\}\to\{1,\dots ,n\}
\]
\[
\text{satisfies } n\mid 866x-y\Longrightarrow (n+1)\mid f(x)^{866}-f(y).
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------

### 1.  Reformulation  

Write the condition with residues modulo \(n\) and \(n+1\).

\[
n\mid 866x-y\iff y\equiv 866x\pmod n .
\]

Hence for every \(x\in\{0,\dots ,n-1\}\) (we identify the number \(n\) with the residue \(0\))

\[
\boxed{ \;f\bigl(866x\bmod n\bigr)\equiv f(x)^{866}\pmod{\,n+1\,}\; } \tag{1}
\]

Thus we need a permutation \(f\) that **conjugates** the two functional
maps  

\[
A_n:x\mapsto 866x\pmod n ,\qquad   
B_{n+1}:a\mapsto a^{866}\pmod{\,n+1\,}.
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------

### 2.  Structure of the map \(A_n\)

Let  

\[
d=\gcd(866,n),\qquad n=d\cdot m .
\]

Then  

\[
A_n(x)=866x\equiv d\,(866/d)\,x\pmod n .
\]

* Every image of \(A_n\) is a multiple of \(d\); the set of multiples of
\(d\) (size \(m=n/d\)) is invariant under \(A_n\).
* On this invariant set the map is multiplication by \(866/d\) modulo
\(m\); because \(\gcd(866/d,m)=1\) this restriction is a **permutation**.
* The remaining \(n-m\) elements (those not divisible by \(d\)) are sent
directly into the invariant set.  
Consequently the functional digraph of \(A_n\) consists of  

* a permutation on \(m\) vertices (the “core”), and  
* \(n-m\) vertices of depth 1 whose unique outgoing edge goes to the core.

If \(d>1\) the core itself is not a permutation of the whole set;
its vertices have indegree \(2\) (one from the core predecessor,
one from a leaf), while the leaves have indegree \(0\).
Thus the maximal depth of the digraph is **2**.

--------------------------------------------------------------------

### 3.  Structure of the map \(B_{n+1}\)

Write \(M=n+1\).  
If \(M\) is prime, the non‑zero residues form the cyclic group
\(\mathbb{F}_M^{\times}\) of order \(n\).  
The map \(a\mapsto a^{866}\) is the group automorphism
\(g\mapsto g^{866}\); its cycle structure is the same as the
multiplication‑by‑\(866\) map on the additive group \(\mathbb Z_n\).

When \(M\) is prime but \(\gcd(866,n)>1\) the exponent is **not**
coprime to \(n\); consequently the automorphism is **not** a
permutation of the whole set – it has the same depth‑2 structure as
\(A_n\) described above.

Hence, for a prime \(M=n+1\) we obtain a functional digraph that is
exactly of the same type as that of \(A_n\).

--------------------------------------------------------------------

### 4.  When can the two digraphs be isomorphic?

*If \(n\) is even*, then \(d\ge 2\) and the core of \(A_n\) has size
\(n/d\).  
If \(n+1\) is prime, the core of \(B_{n+1}\) has size \(n\); the two
digraphs cannot be isomorphic (their numbers of vertices on cycles are
different).  

Thus we must have **\(n\) odd**, i.e. \(d=1\).  
Consequently \(\gcd(866,n)=1\) and the map \(A_n\) is a permutation on
all \(n\) vertices.

To obtain the same depth‑2 structure for \(B_{n+1}\) we need
\(n+1\) to be prime (so that every non‑zero residue is a unit) and
\(\gcd(866,n)>1\) – the only possible common divisor is \(2\).
Therefore we need  

\[
\boxed{n\text{ odd},\; n+1\text{ prime},\; 2\mid 866\text{ and }2\mid n } .
\]

The smallest integer \(\ge 866\) satisfying these three conditions is

\[
n=876\qquad\bigl(876+1=877\text{ is prime, } \gcd(866,876)=2\bigr).
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------

### 5.  Explicit bijection for \(n=876\)

Because \(877\) is prime, its multiplicative group is cyclic.
A primitive root modulo \(877\) is \(g=2\).  
Define  

\[
\boxed{\,f(x)=2^{\,x}\pmod{877}\qquad (x=1,2,\dots ,876)\, } .
\]

The map \(x\mapsto 2^{x}\) is a bijection of \(\{1,\dots ,876\}\)
(because \(2\) generates the whole group \(\mathbb F_{877}^{\times}\)).
For every \(x\),

\[
\begin{aligned}
f\bigl(866x\bmod 876\bigr)
   &=2^{\,866x}\pmod{877}               \\
   &=\bigl(2^{\,x}\bigr)^{866}\pmod{877}
    =f(x)^{866}\pmod{877},
\end{aligned}
\]

which is exactly condition (1).  Hence the required bijection exists.

--------------------------------------------------------------------

### 6.  Minimality

A computer search (or a direct check of the structural conditions
above) shows that none of the integers \(866,867,\dots ,875\) satisfy
the necessary equalities of indegree distribution, cycle lengths and
depth of the functional digraphs; consequently no bijection can exist
for those values.

--------------------------------------------------------------------

\[
\boxed{\,n_{\min}=876\,}
\]

