        The centre of the sphere must stay at least a distance  

\[
r=\frac{\sqrt6}{36}
\]

from each face of the regular tetrahedron of side \(a=1\).
For a convex polyhedron the set of points whose distance to every face is
\(\ge r\) is the intersection of the half‑spaces obtained by moving each
face inward by \(r\).  
Since \(r\) is smaller than the inradius, this intersection is again a
regular tetrahedron, similar to the original one and sharing the same
centroid.

---

### 1.  Inradius of the original tetrahedron  

For a regular tetrahedron of side \(a\),

\[
r_{\text{in}}=\frac{a\sqrt6}{12}.
\]

With \(a=1\),

\[
r_{\text{in}}=\frac{\sqrt6}{12}.
\]

The given sphere radius is  

\[
r=\frac{\sqrt6}{36}= \frac{1}{3}\,r_{\text{in}} .
\]

---

### 2.  Scaling factor  

The distance from the centroid to each face is reduced from \(r_{\text{in}}\)
to \(r_{\text{in}}-r\).  
Because all linear dimensions of a regular tetrahedron are proportional to
that distance, the side length scales by  

\[
s=\frac{r_{\text{in}}-r}{r_{\text{in}}}
   =\frac{\frac{\sqrt6}{12}-\frac{\sqrt6}{36}}{\frac{\sqrt6}{12}}
   =\frac{1/12-1/36}{1/12}= \frac{2}{3}.
\]

Hence the inner tetrahedron has side length  

\[
a' = s a = \frac{2}{3}.
\]

---

### 3.  Volume of the reachable region  

The volume of a regular tetrahedron of side \(a\) is  

\[
V(a)=\frac{a^{3}}{6\sqrt2}.
\]

Thus  

\[
V = V(1)\,s^{3}
   =\frac{1}{6\sqrt2}\left(\frac{2}{3}\right)^{3}
   =\frac{1}{6\sqrt2}\cdot\frac{8}{27}
   =\frac{4}{81\sqrt2}
   =\frac{2\sqrt2}{81}.
\]

---

### 4.  Required integer  

\[
V^{2}= \left(\frac{2\sqrt2}{81}\right)^{2}= \frac{8}{6561},
\qquad
\frac{1}{V^{2}} = \frac{6561}{8}=820.125.
\]

Therefore  

\[
\boxed{\left\lfloor\frac{1}{V^{2}}\right\rfloor = 820 }.
\]

