        [Turn 9 REASONING] (2363 chars)
        The relation  

\[
x\succ y\iff 0<x-y\le 9\ \text{or}\ y-x>9
\]

can be rewritten modulo \(19\).

---

### 1.  A cyclic tournament  

For \(x,y\in\{1,\dots ,19\}\) put  

\[
d\equiv x-y\pmod{19},\qquad 0\le d\le 18 .
\]

Then  

\[
x\succ y\iff 1\le d\le 9 .
\]

Thus the vertices \(1,\dots ,19\) form a **circulant tournament**:  
vertex \(x\) beats exactly the next nine vertices
\(x+1,\dots ,x+9\) (indices taken modulo \(19\)).  
Every ordered pair of distinct vertices is comparable, and the tournament is
regular (out‑degree \(=9\)).

A four‑tuple \((a,b,c,d)\) satisfying  

\[
a\succ b,\;b\succ c,\;c\succ d,\;d\succ a
\]

is precisely a directed 4‑cycle in this tournament.

---

### 2.  Describing a cycle by “steps”

Write  

\[
b\equiv a+s_{1},\;c\equiv b+s_{2},\;d\equiv c+s_{3},\;
a\equiv d+s_{4}\pmod{19},
\]

where each step \(s_i\) is one of the nine winning differences:

\[
s_i\in\{1,2,\dots ,9\}\qquad(i=1,\dots ,4).
\]

Adding the four congruences gives  

\[
s_{1}+s_{2}+s_{3}+s_{4}\equiv 0\pmod{19}.
\]

Because each \(s_i\le 9\), the total sum lies between \(4\) and \(36\); the
only multiple of \(19\) in this interval is \(19\). Hence a directed
4‑cycle must satisfy  

\[
s_{1}+s_{2}+s_{3}+s_{4}=19. \tag{1}
\]

If (1) holds, the partial sums  

\[
s_{1},\;s_{1}+s_{2},\;s_{1}+s_{2}+s_{3}
\]

are all \(<19\); therefore none of the intermediate vertices coincides with
the starting vertex, and the four vertices are distinct.  
Thus every solution of (1) yields a valid ordered 4‑tuple, and conversely
every ordered 4‑tuple gives a solution of (1).

---

### 3.  Counting the step‑tuples

The number of ordered quadruples \((s_{1},s_{2},s_{3},s_{4})\) with
\(s_i\in\{1,\dots ,9\}\) and sum \(19\) is the coefficient of \(x^{19}\) in  

\[
\bigl(x+x^{2}+\dots +x^{9}\bigr)^{4}.
\]

Using a simple expansion (or a short program) we obtain  

\[
[x^{19}]\bigl(x+x^{2}+\dots +x^{9}\bigr)^{4}=480 .
\]

Hence there are \(480\) possible step‑patterns.

---

### 4.  Choosing the starting vertex

The starting vertex \(a\) can be any of the \(19\) numbers.
For each of the \(480\) step‑patterns we obtain a distinct ordered
four‑tuple \((a,b,c,d)\).

\[
\boxed{19\times 480 = 9120}
\]

