        The relation \(x\succ y\) means  

\[
x\succ y\iff 0<x-y\le 9\quad\text{or}\quad y-x>9 .
\]

For numbers \(1,\dots ,19\) this is equivalent to  

\[
x\succ y\iff (x-y)\pmod{19}\in\{1,2,\dots ,9\},
\]

i.e. each element beats the next nine elements on the circle of length \(19\).

---

### Step representation  

Let  

\[
s_{1}=b-a,\; s_{2}=c-b,\; s_{3}=d-c,\; s_{4}=a-d\pmod{19}.
\]

Then \(a\succ b,\;b\succ c,\;c\succ d,\;d\succ a\) is exactly

\[
s_{1},s_{2},s_{3},s_{4}\in\{1,\dots ,9\}\quad\text{and}\quad
s_{1}+s_{2}+s_{3}+s_{4}\equiv0\pmod{19}.
\]

Because each \(s_i\le 9\), the sum satisfies \(4\le s_{1}+s_{2}+s_{3}+s_{4}\le 36\); the only multiple of \(19\) in this interval is \(19\).  
Hence we need

\[
s_{1}+s_{2}+s_{3}+s_{4}=19,\qquad 1\le s_i\le 9 .
\]

---

### Counting the step quadruples  

Set \(x_i=s_i-1\;(i=1,\dots ,4)\).  
Then \(x_i\ge0,\;x_i\le8\) and  

\[
x_{1}+x_{2}+x_{3}+x_{4}=15 .
\]

Without the upper bound the number of non‑negative solutions is  

\[
\binom{15+4-1}{4-1}= \binom{18}{3}=816 .
\]

Subtract those where some \(x_i\ge9\).  
For a fixed \(i\), put \(y_i=x_i-9\ge0\); the remaining sum is \(15-9=6\), giving  

\[
\binom{6+4-1}{3}= \binom{9}{3}=84
\]

solutions. There are \(4\) choices of the index, so \(4\cdot84=336\) are removed.  
Two variables cannot both be \(\ge9\) because that would require a sum at least \(18\) exceeding \(15\). Hence no further inclusion–exclusion terms appear.

\[
\text{Number of step quadruples}=816-336=480 .
\]

---

### From steps to actual 4‑tuples  

For any admissible step quadruple \((s_1,s_2,s_3,s_4)\) we may choose any starting value \(a\in\{1,\dots ,19\}\); the other three numbers are then uniquely determined (mod 19) and are distinct (the only possible repetition would require a sum of three steps equal to \(19\), which cannot happen because each step is at least \(1\)).  

Thus each step quadruple yields exactly \(19\) ordered 4‑tuples \((a,b,c,d)\).

\[
\boxed{19 \times 480 = 9120}
\]

Hence there are **9120** four‑tuples \((a,b,c,d)\) satisfying the given cyclic “\(\succ\)” conditions.

