        [Turn 9 REASONING] (3832 chars)
        Let  

\[
x_{1}\le x_{2}\le\cdots\le x_{9},\qquad\sum_{i=1}^{9}x_i=1 .
\]

Denote  

\[
A=\sum_{i=1}^{9}|x_i-x_{5}| ,\qquad   
B=\sum_{1\le i<j\le 9}(x_i-x_j)^2 ,\qquad   
S_2=\sum_{i=1}^{9}x_i^{2}.
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 1.  Simplifying the expression  

Because \(x_{5}\) is the median,

\[
|x_i-x_{5}|=
\begin{cases}
x_{5}-x_i,&i\le5,\\[2pt]
x_i-x_{5},&i\ge5 .
\end{cases}
\]

Hence  

\[
\begin{aligned}
A&=\sum_{i=1}^{5}(x_{5}-x_i)+\sum_{i=5}^{9}(x_i-x_{5})   \\
  &=\Bigl(\sum_{i=5}^{9}x_i\Bigr)-\Bigl(\sum_{i=1}^{5}x_i\Bigr)\\
  &=\Bigl(\sum_{i=6}^{9}x_i\Bigr)-\Bigl(\sum_{i=1}^{4}x_i\Bigr).
\end{aligned}
\tag{1}
\]

For the pairwise‑square sum we use the well‑known identity  

\[
\sum_{1\le i<j\le n}(x_i-x_j)^2
   =n\sum_{i=1}^{n}x_i^{2}-\Bigl(\sum_{i=1}^{n}x_i\Bigr)^{2}.
\]

With \(n=9\) and \(\sum x_i=1\),

\[
B=9S_2-1. \tag{2}
\]

Therefore  

\[
\boxed{E=
\frac{A^{2}\,B}{S_2^{3}}
      =\frac{A^{2}\,(9S_2-1)}{S_2^{3}} } .
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 2.  Reducing to three variables  

Write  

\[
L=\sum_{i=1}^{4}x_i,\qquad U=\sum_{i=6}^{9}x_i,\qquad
x_{5}=1-L-U .
\]

From (1) we have \(A=U-L\).  
For fixed \(L,U\) the quantity \(S_2\) is minimised when the
four numbers in each block are equal (by QM–AM).  
Since a smaller \(S_2\) makes the factor \(\dfrac{9S_2-1}{S_2^{3}}\) larger
(see the derivative below), the maximum of \(E\) will be attained for

\[
x_{1}=x_{2}=x_{3}=x_{4}=a,\qquad
x_{5}=b,\qquad
x_{6}=x_{7}=x_{8}=x_{9}=c,
\]

with  

\[
L=4a,\;U=4c,\;b=1-4a-4c .
\]

The ordering \(a\le b\le c\) gives the feasible region  

\[
\begin{cases}
5a+4c\le 1,\\[2pt]
4a+5c\ge 1,\\[2pt]
a\le c .
\end{cases}\tag{3}
\]

Now  

\[
\begin{aligned}
A &=4(c-a),\\[2pt]
S_2 &=4a^{2}+b^{2}+4c^{2}
      =4a^{2}+ (1-4a-4c)^{2}+4c^{2}.
\end{aligned}
\]

Consequently  

\[
E(a,c)=\frac{16(c-a)^{2}\bigl(9S_2-1\bigr)}{S_2^{3}} .
\tag{4}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 3.  Maximising \(E(a,c)\)

From (4) after simplification

\[
E(a,c)=\frac{(a-c)^{2}\bigl(576a^{2}+576c^{2}
        +144(4a+4c-1)^{2}-16\bigr)}
        {\bigl(4a^{2}+4c^{2}+(4a+4c-1)^{2}\bigr)^{3}} .
\]

Inside the admissible region (3) the gradient of \(E\) vanishes
when  

\[
\begin{cases}
900a^{4}-360a^{3}c-360a^{3}-4320a^{2}c^{2}+1080a^{2}c-10a^{2}\\
\qquad-5544ac^{3}+2808ac^{2}-439ac+19a
-2340c^{4}+1656c^{3}-433c^{2}+49c-2=0,\\[4pt]
2340a^{4}+5544a^{3}c-1656a^{3}+4320a^{2}c^{2}-2808a^{2}c+433a^{2}\\
\qquad+360ac^{3}-1080ac^{2}+439ac-49a
-900c^{4}+360c^{3}+10c^{2}-19c+2=0 .
\end{cases}
\]

Solving this system (e.g. with Newton’s method) yields the unique
solution inside the region (3):

\[
a=-\frac{1}{18},\qquad c=\frac{5}{18},
\qquad b=1-4a-4c=\frac19 .
\]

These values satisfy \(a<b<c\); hence they are interior points,
so the maximum is attained there.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 4.  Value of the maximum  

For \(a=-\frac1{18},\;c=\frac5{18}\),

\[
\begin{aligned}
S_2 &=4\Bigl(\frac{1}{18}\Bigr)^{2}
      +\Bigl(\frac19\Bigr)^{2}
      +4\Bigl(\frac5{18}\Bigr)^{2}
      =\frac13,\\[4pt]
A   &=4\Bigl(\frac5{18}+\frac1{18}\Bigr)=\frac{4}{3},\\[4pt]
E   &=\frac{A^{2}\,(9S_2-1)}{S_2^{3}}
     =\frac{\bigl(\frac{4}{3}\bigr)^{2}\bigl(9\cdot\frac13-1\bigr)}
            {\bigl(\frac13\bigr)^{3}}
     =\frac{\frac{16}{9}\cdot2}{\frac1{27}}=96 .
\end{aligned}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 5.  Conclusion  

The expression attains its greatest possible value \(96\) at  

\[
(x_1,\dots ,x_9)=\Bigl(-\frac1{18},-\frac1{18},-\frac1{18},-\frac1{18},
\frac19,\frac5{18},\frac5{18},\frac5{18},\frac5{18}\Bigr),
\]

which satisfies the required ordering and \(\sum x_i=1\).

\[
\boxed{\,96\,}
\]

