We'll store factors as dict mapping frozenset of variable indices to numpy array.

Initially, we have binary factors for each edge (u,v) with matrix M_uv.

We can also have unary factors (all ones) which we can ignore.

We'll eliminate variables in the given order (order list). At each step, we collect all factors that involve the variable v. Multiply them (tensor product) to get a joint factor over the union of variables. Then sum over v's domain (size 18) to produce a new factor over remaining variables.

Multiplying factors: if we have multiple binary factors sharing variable v, we need to multiply them pointwise over shared dimensions. This can be done by aligning axes.

Implementation details: Represent factor as numpy array with axes ordered according to list of variable IDs (sorted maybe). We'll maintain mapping from variable set to factor array and variable ordering list.

When eliminating v, we collect factors where v in set. Compute combined factor by successive tensor multiplication (outer product) and then elementwise multiplication aligning dimensions.

Simplify: Since all factors are binary (pairwise) and we eliminate one variable at a time, the combined factor will be over v and its neighbor variables that are still present. The degree of v in the current graph (after previous eliminations) may increase due to fill edges, but we can compute.

We'll implement elimination algorithm using dictionary of factors.

